    "reasoning": "Using AWS Lambda for orchestration allows for serverless execution, which scales automatically and is cost-effective for sporadic, scheduled jobs. Amazon ECS is utilized to manage the batch job processing, allowing multiple containers to handle data in parallel while maintaining zero-downtime through blue/green deployments. The combination of these services ensures that the reconciliation job can execute without impacting other operations and can scale as required based on the dataset size. \n\nAmazon RDS is chosen for relational data storage, providing high availability and durability for financial data. Amazon S3 serves as a staging area for incoming datasets, which allows for easy data retrieval and processing without interfering with the database. The use of Amazon SQS provides reliable messaging to decouple the job execution from data processing, further enhancing reliability and ensuring that the system can handle spikes in demand without data loss."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
      "chose": "Amazon ECS",
      "reason": "Amazon ECS is better suited for processing large datasets like 50GB due to its ability to manage resource-intensive tasks without timeout issues.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the processing requirements decrease significantly or if the job can be split into smaller, short-duration tasks."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon RDS vs Amazon DynamoDB",
      "chose": "Amazon RDS",
      "reason": "Amazon RDS provides the necessary SQL capabilities and transactions required for financial data integrity, which DynamoDB does not support natively.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the application demands high-throughput and flexible scaling beyond the capabilities of RDS."
    },
    {
      "decision": "AWS Application Load Balancer vs AWS Network Load Balancer",
      "chose": "AWS Application Load Balancer",
      "reason": "AWS Application Load Balancer supports advanced routing capabilities and is better suited for HTTP traffic, which allows for seamless service integration.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the architecture needs to scale to millions of requests per second requiring TCP/UDP support."
    }
  ],
  "cost": {
