    "reasoning": "For this document processing pipeline, using Amazon S3 provides a robust and cost-effective storage solution for the input PDFs and processed files. AWS Lambda allows for an event-driven architecture where each PDF processing task can be handled asynchronously, ensuring scalability as the load increases. Amazon ECS can be used for more complex processing tasks that require container orchestration, offering flexibility and the ability to manage microservices effectively.\n\nAmazon DynamoDB serves as a highly available NoSQL database for storing metadata and processing status, while Amazon RDS can be leveraged for any relational data needs, such as user information or logs. The combination of Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS allows for a reliable messaging system that decouples the components of the pipeline, enhancing reliability and fault tolerance. The architecture is designed for scalability, as services like Lambda scale automatically with demand, and the use of CloudWatch enables monitoring of performance metrics to optimize resource usage and costs."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
      "chose": "AWS Lambda",
      "reason": "AWS Lambda is ideal for event-driven workloads and can handle spikes in traffic without pre-provisioning servers.",
      "when_to_switch": "If processing logic becomes too complex or requires long-running processes, transitioning to Amazon ECS may be more suitable."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon S3 vs Amazon EFS",
      "chose": "Amazon S3",
      "reason": "Amazon S3 provides lower storage costs and better durability for large volumes of static files like PDFs.",
