      "messaging": [
        "Amazon SQS"
      ],
      "monitoring": [
        "Amazon CloudWatch",
        "AWS X-Ray"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "For the compute layer, AWS Lambda allows for serverless execution of CI/CD processes, providing automatic scaling to handle peak loads from multiple teams. Amazon EC2 and Amazon ECS can be leveraged for more persistent workloads and containerized applications, respectively, ensuring flexibility and ease of management. This combination allows the architecture to scale horizontally as needed, accommodating the varying demands of 500 engineering teams.\n\nIn the database layer, Amazon RDS provides a managed relational database solution for storing configuration and build data, while Amazon DynamoDB offers a NoSQL option for high-speed access to ephemeral data generated during build and deployment processes. This ensures that the system remains reliable and responsive even under heavy load. The messaging layer with Amazon SQS further enhances reliability by decoupling components, allowing for asynchronous processing of tasks and preventing bottlenecks in the CI/CD pipeline.",
    "trade_offs": [
      {
        "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon EC2 for compute",
        "chose": "AWS Lambda",
        "reason": "AWS Lambda is more cost-effective for event-driven tasks, automatically scaling with usage and allowing for a pay-as-you-go model.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the workloads become consistently predictable and require long-running processes, Amazon EC2 may be more suitable."
      },
      {
        "decision": "Amazon RDS vs Amazon DynamoDB for database",
        "chose": "Amazon RDS",
        "reason": "Amazon RDS is better suited for structured data and complex queries required by CI/CD tools, while still being managed and scalable.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the data access patterns shift towards unstructured data, Amazon DynamoDB would be a better fit due to its low-latency performance."
      },
      {
        "decision": "AWS Application Load Balancer vs Amazon API Gateway",
        "chose": "AWS Application Load Balancer",
        "reason": "The Application Load Balancer provides advanced routing capabilities that are ideal for distributing traffic across multiple services in a CI/CD pipeline.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the architecture requires more complex API management features, Amazon API Gateway would be preferable."
      }
    ],
    "cost": {
