    "reasoning": "For object storage, Amazon S3 is the ideal choice due to its durability, scalability, and cost-effectiveness. It can handle large volumes of unstructured data like video files and offers lifecycle policies to manage costs by transitioning data to lower-cost storage classes as it ages. Using Amazon CloudFront as a CDN will ensure that the video content is delivered with low latency and high transfer speeds to users globally, reducing the load on the S3 bucket and optimizing data transfer costs. AWS Direct Connect can be used to establish a dedicated network connection to AWS, which can reduce data transfer costs and provide a more consistent network experience, especially for large data transfers from on-premises to AWS or vice versa. This combination ensures that the architecture is both reliable and efficient, minimizing costs while maximizing performance.",
    "trade_offs": [
      {
        "decision": "ECS (Fargate) vs EC2 for compute",
        "chose": "ECS (Fargate)",
        "reason": "Fargate abstracts away the infrastructure management, allowing the client to focus on deploying and managing their applications without worrying about the underlying servers. This is particularly beneficial for a media company that may have fluctuating workloads.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the client requires more control over the underlying infrastructure or needs to run applications that require specific EC2 instance types or configurations, switching to EC2 might be necessary."
      },
      {
        "decision": "S3 Standard vs S3 Intelligent-Tiering",
        "chose": "S3 Intelligent-Tiering",
        "reason": "S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves data between two access tiers when access patterns change, which can help optimize costs for video files that may not be accessed frequently after initial upload.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the client has predictable access patterns and can manage lifecycle policies effectively, S3 Standard may be more cost-effective."
      },
      {
        "decision": "Using CloudFront vs Direct S3 access",
        "chose": "CloudFront",
        "reason": "Using CloudFront as a CDN reduces latency and improves load times for end users by caching content at edge locations, which is critical for a media company that serves video content globally.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the client has a limited budget and can tolerate higher latency, direct access to S3 may be considered, but this could impact user experience."
      }
    ]
  },
  "trade_offs": [],
