      "monitoring": [
        "Amazon CloudWatch",
        "AWS X-Ray"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "The architecture leverages Amazon CloudFront to cache static content at edge locations, minimizing latency for users. The API Gateway serves as the entry point for all client requests, allowing for throttling and monitoring. AWS Lambda is used for serverless, scalable compute, enabling quick scaling and reducing costs during low traffic. For heavier processing tasks, Amazon ECS can handle containerized workloads. \n\nFor data storage, Amazon DynamoDB is chosen for its low-latency performance and scalability, perfect for the high-speed requirements of a recommendation engine. Amazon ElastiCache is utilized to cache frequently accessed data, further reducing response times. The use of Amazon SQS helps decouple components, allowing for reliable message queuing while maintaining system resilience and throughput. Overall, this architecture ensures high availability, low latency, and cost-effectiveness, meeting the client's requirement for sub-50ms response times."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
      "chose": "AWS Lambda",
      "reason": "AWS Lambda provides a serverless approach that scales automatically and charges only for compute time, making it cost-effective for variable workloads.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the compute requirements exceed the limits of AWS Lambda, such as long-running processes, then Amazon ECS would be a better fit."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon DynamoDB vs Amazon Aurora",
      "chose": "Amazon DynamoDB",
      "reason": "Amazon DynamoDB offers low-latency performance and scales seamlessly with demand, which is crucial for real-time recommendations.",
      "when_to_switch": "If complex queries or transactions become necessary, Amazon Aurora could be better suited due to its relational capabilities."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon SQS vs Amazon Kinesis",
      "chose": "Amazon SQS",
      "reason": "Amazon SQS is simpler and sufficient for decoupling the architecture with guaranteed message delivery without the need for real-time processing.",
      "when_to_switch": "If real-time data streaming becomes a requirement, Amazon Kinesis would be more appropriate for handling continuous data streams."
    }
  ],
  "cost": {
