        "Amazon SQS"
      ],
      "monitoring": [
        "Amazon CloudWatch",
        "AWS X-Ray"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "The architecture leverages Amazon CloudFront as a content delivery network (CDN) to cache and deliver content closer to users, reducing latency and improving performance. AWS Global Accelerator provides static IP addresses and enhances availability by routing traffic to the optimal endpoint based on health and performance. Elastic Load Balancing distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, ensuring high availability and fault tolerance. Amazon Route 53 is used for highly available DNS services with health checks to direct traffic only to healthy endpoints, further enhancing resilience against DDoS attacks.

For compute resources, Amazon EC2 instances can be used to host the application, while AWS Lambda can handle serverless workloads, providing scalability and cost-effectiveness. Amazon RDS and DynamoDB serve as the database layer, offering managed database services that can scale according to demand. Amazon SQS is included for decoupling components of the architecture, allowing for asynchronous processing of requests. This architecture is designed to be highly resilient and scalable, ensuring that the government website can withstand potential DDoS attacks while maintaining performance and availability."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Shield Standard vs AWS Shield Advanced",
      "chose": "AWS Shield Advanced",
      "reason": "Given the high-profile nature of the government website, AWS Shield Advanced provides enhanced protection against larger and more sophisticated DDoS attacks, including application layer attacks, which is critical for maintaining uptime and security.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the budget constraints become a significant concern, AWS Shield Standard may be considered for basic protection against common DDoS attacks."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon EC2 vs AWS Lambda",
      "chose": "Amazon EC2",
      "reason": "Amazon EC2 provides more control over the environment and is suitable for running complex applications that require persistent state and custom configurations, which may be necessary for a government website.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the application can be broken down into microservices with stateless functions, AWS Lambda could be more cost-effective and easier to scale."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon RDS vs Amazon DynamoDB",
      "chose": "Amazon RDS",
      "reason": "Amazon RDS is chosen for its support of complex queries and transactions, which may be necessary for the relational data model of the government website, ensuring data integrity and consistency.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the application requires high scalability with a flexible schema and can tolerate eventual consistency, Amazon DynamoDB would be a better fit."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Elastic Load Balancing vs AWS Global Accelerator",
      "chose": "AWS Global Accelerator",
      "reason": "AWS Global Accelerator provides improved availability and performance by routing traffic to the optimal endpoint based on health and latency, which is crucial for a high-profile government website that requires consistent performance.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the architecture needs to simplify routing and load balancing for a smaller scale application, Elastic Load Balancing could be sufficient."
    }
  ],
