        "Amazon CloudWatch"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "For the edge layer, Amazon CloudFront improves the performance of the application by caching content closer to the users, which is crucial for a high-traffic platform like a hotel booking system. The networking layer includes Amazon API Gateway for managing APIs and Elastic Load Balancing to distribute incoming traffic across multiple compute resources, ensuring high availability. The use of AWS Lambda allows for a serverless compute option that can scale automatically based on the request volume, making it highly cost-effective for variable workloads. Furthermore, Amazon ECS can be utilized for containerized workloads that might require more control over the environment.\n\nIn the database layer, Amazon Aurora is an excellent choice due to its high performance and scalability, supporting up to 64TB of data, which is essential for handling large volumes of transactions. Coupled with Amazon ElastiCache, which provides in-memory caching, the architecture can deliver low-latency responses for frequently accessed data, significantly enhancing user experience. Amazon SQS is included for decoupling components of the architecture, allowing for better management of transactional workflows and ensuring reliability during peak loads. Monitoring with Amazon CloudWatch will provide insights into application performance and resource utilization, enabling proactive management of the infrastructure."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
      "chose": "AWS Lambda",
      "reason": "AWS Lambda is serverless and automatically scales with demand, making it cost-effective for variable workloads without the need to manage servers.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the application requires long-running processes or specific runtime environments that Lambda does not support, Amazon ECS would be a better fit."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon Aurora vs Amazon RDS",
      "chose": "Amazon Aurora",
      "reason": "Amazon Aurora offers superior performance and scalability compared to Amazon RDS, ideal for handling high transaction volumes, and has a cost-effective pricing model for high workloads.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the application has lower database performance requirements, or if it is a smaller-scale application, Amazon RDS could suffice and reduce costs."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon SQS vs Amazon SNS",
      "chose": "Amazon SQS",
      "reason": "Amazon SQS provides a reliable messaging queue that helps decouple services and manage transactions efficiently, ensuring that messages are processed in the order received.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the architecture needs to push notifications to multiple subscribers in real time, Amazon SNS would be more appropriate."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Elastic Load Balancing vs Amazon CloudFront",
      "chose": "Elastic Load Balancing",
      "reason": "Elastic Load Balancing directly manages and distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, providing a seamless experience for users.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the primary concern is static content delivery and performance optimization, Amazon CloudFront would be better suited for that purpose."
    }
  ],
