    "reasoning": "The architecture leverages Amazon CloudFront as a CDN to cache and serve static content, ensuring low latency for users during the migration. An Application Load Balancer (ALB) is used to distribute incoming traffic across multiple ECS services, allowing for gradual traffic shifting between the old and new database systems. Amazon ECS with Fargate is chosen for its serverless compute capabilities, enabling easy scaling and management of containerized applications without the need for provisioning or managing servers. This setup provides high availability and resilience, as Fargate automatically handles the scaling of containers based on demand.

For the database layer, Amazon RDS is selected due to its managed nature, allowing for automated backups, scaling, and failover capabilities. The Multi-AZ deployment ensures high availability during the migration process. To facilitate communication between services and manage the migration process, Amazon SQS is used for decoupling the services, ensuring that messages are reliably delivered even if one of the services is temporarily unavailable. Monitoring through Amazon CloudWatch allows for real-time insights into application performance and health, enabling proactive management of the migration process."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "ECS (Fargate) vs EC2",
      "chose": "ECS (Fargate)",
      "reason": "Fargate allows for serverless container management, reducing operational overhead and simplifying scaling without needing to manage EC2 instances.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the workload requires specific EC2 instance types for performance optimization or if there are complex networking requirements that Fargate cannot accommodate."
