    "reasoning": "Using Amazon CloudFront as a CDN will ensure low latency and fast content delivery across different geographic regions, improving the user experience for real estate listings. The combination of AWS Lambda for serverless compute and Amazon ECS for containerized workloads allows the architecture to scale seamlessly with varying loads, which is essential given the high number of transactions. This serverless approach helps keep costs low by charging only for the compute time consumed, while ECS can handle more complex processing when needed.\n\nFor the database layer, Amazon RDS provides a relational database solution that can be scaled and managed easily, while Amazon DynamoDB offers a NoSQL option for high-speed transactions and flexible data models. This hybrid approach allows the application to utilize the strengths of both types of databases, facilitating efficient data management and retrieval. The use of Amazon SQS for messaging ensures reliable communication between microservices, enhancing the overall reliability of the architecture.\n\nMonitoring with Amazon CloudWatch will provide insights into application performance and operational health, enabling proactive management of resources and costs as the application grows.",
    "trade_offs": [
      {
        "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
        "chose": "AWS Lambda",
        "reason": "AWS Lambda provides a cost-effective, serverless solution that scales automatically with the number of transactions without the need to manage servers.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the application requires long-running processes or specific configurations that Lambda cannot provide, switching to Amazon ECS would be more suitable."
      },
      {
        "decision": "Amazon RDS vs Amazon DynamoDB",
        "chose": "Amazon RDS",
        "reason": "Amazon RDS is ideal for structured data and complex queries, which are common in real estate applications, ensuring data integrity and ACID compliance.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the application needs to scale massively with very high read/write throughput, Amazon DynamoDB could be more efficient."
      },
      {
        "decision": "Amazon API Gateway vs Elastic Load Balancing",
        "chose": "Amazon API Gateway",
        "reason": "Amazon API Gateway is better suited for handling RESTful APIs and offers built-in monitoring and security features, which streamline development and operations.",
        "when_to_switch": "If the application requires load balancing for non-API traffic or needs custom routing, Elastic Load Balancing would be a better fit."
      }
