      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "For a high-throughput CI/CD platform, AWS Fargate is chosen to run containerized applications without the need for managing servers, allowing for seamless scaling based on demand. AWS Lambda is also included for executing serverless functions that can quickly respond to events, such as code commits or build triggers. This combination provides flexibility and ensures that the system can handle varying workloads efficiently.\n\nIn the database layer, Amazon DynamoDB is selected for its ability to scale horizontally while providing low-latency access to data, which is critical for CI/CD operations. Amazon RDS can be used for relational data needs, enabling efficient management of build and deployment metadata. By using Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS, we can facilitate messaging between different components, ensuring that build processes can trigger notifications or further actions without creating bottlenecks.\n\nThe use of Amazon CloudFront as an edge service improves the performance of static assets and reduces latency for users accessing the CI/CD dashboard or artifacts. Moreover, the AWS Application Load Balancer ensures high availability and distributes traffic effectively across multiple instances, supporting the high throughput requirement."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Fargate vs Amazon EC2",
      "chose": "AWS Fargate",
      "reason": "Fargate abstracts server management, allowing teams to focus on deployment rather than infrastructure, which is ideal for rapid CI/CD cycles.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the organization needs more control over the underlying infrastructure or requires specific optimizations that Fargate cannot provide."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon DynamoDB vs Amazon RDS",
      "chose": "Amazon DynamoDB",
      "reason": "DynamoDB offers high scalability and low latency, which are essential for real-time CI/CD operations, handling large volumes of data efficiently.",
      "when_to_switch": "If complex queries or transactions are needed that DynamoDB cannot handle effectively."
    },
    {
      "decision": "AWS Application Load Balancer vs Network Load Balancer",
      "chose": "AWS Application Load Balancer",
      "reason": "The Application Load Balancer is better suited for HTTP/HTTPS traffic and provides advanced routing capabilities for the CI/CD platform.",
      "when_to_switch": "If there is a need for handling TCP traffic or if very low latency is a critical requirement."
    }
  ],
  "cost": {
    "monthly_breakdown": [
      {
        "service": "Amazon CloudFront",
        "monthly_usd": 150.0,
        "unit": "data transfer and requests"
      },
      {
        "service": "AWS Fargate",
