        "Amazon CloudWatch"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "Using Amazon CloudFront as a CDN enhances the performance of the logistics platform by caching content closer to users, which can reduce latency and improve load times. The architecture utilizes Amazon API Gateway to manage and route requests efficiently, while the Application Load Balancer distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, ensuring high availability and reliability. AWS Lambda is chosen for its scalability, as it allows the platform to process records without provisioning or managing servers, while Amazon ECS supports containerized applications handling more complex workloads when needed.\n\nFor the database, Amazon DynamoDB is an excellent choice due to its fully managed nature, providing fast and predictable performance with seamless scalability. This can easily accommodate the growth of the logistics platform as the number of records and users increases. Amazon SQS is incorporated for decoupling services, ensuring that the logistics platform can handle spikes in traffic by buffering requests, thus improving reliability. Monitoring with Amazon CloudWatch allows the client to gain insights into application performance and operational health, ensuring proactive maintenance and troubleshooting."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
      "chose": "AWS Lambda",
      "reason": "AWS Lambda is serverless, which reduces operational overhead and automatically scales with incoming requests, making it ideal for unpredictable workloads.",
      "when_to_switch": "If there is a need for long-running processes or specific container management features, Amazon ECS would be a better fit."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon DynamoDB vs Amazon RDS",
      "chose": "Amazon DynamoDB",
      "reason": "Amazon DynamoDB offers a NoSQL solution that is fully managed and scales automatically, which is well-suited for the dynamic nature of logistics data.",
      "when_to_switch": "If complex queries and transactions are required, Amazon RDS would be a more appropriate choice."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Application Load Balancer vs Network Load Balancer",
      "chose": "Application Load Balancer",
      "reason": "Application Load Balancer operates at the application layer, enabling advanced routing and SSL termination, which is essential for HTTP/HTTPS traffic.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the logistics platform needs to handle TCP traffic for non-HTTP applications, a Network Load Balancer would be more suitable."
    }
