    "reasoning": "To achieve per-customer data isolation, Amazon RDS can be set up with separate schemas or databases for each tenant, allowing for data security and compliance. Using AWS Lambda for compute enables a serverless architecture that scales automatically with user demand, which is ideal for a B2B SaaS platform where usage may vary significantly between customers. Amazon DynamoDB can also be utilized for high-velocity data, providing low-latency access and seamless scaling. The combination of these services ensures both reliability and efficient handling of varying workloads.\n\nAmazon CloudFront will provide a global content delivery network for serving static assets quickly, enhancing the user experience. API Gateway and Elastic Load Balancing will facilitate smooth traffic distribution and endpoint management, ensuring that APIs are scalable and secure. This architecture is cost-effective as it minimizes the need for overprovisioning resources, allowing the client to pay only for what they use while ensuring high availability and performance."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "Amazon RDS vs Amazon DynamoDB",
      "chose": "Amazon RDS",
      "reason": "Amazon RDS is better suited for complex queries and transactional support, which is often required in multi-tenant applications.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the application requires rapid scalability and high write throughput without complex transactions, Amazon DynamoDB would be more appropriate."
    },
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS",
      "chose": "AWS Lambda",
      "reason": "AWS Lambda offers a serverless option that automatically scales with demand, which is more cost-efficient for sporadic workloads typical in B2B applications.",
      "when_to_switch": "For long-running processes or when more control over the environment is needed, Amazon ECS would be better."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon API Gateway vs AWS Elastic Load Balancing",
      "chose": "Amazon API Gateway",
      "reason": "Amazon API Gateway provides built-in support for RESTful APIs, making it easier to manage and secure the APIs with features like throttling and authorization.",
