      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "To handle the high read QPS, Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL is chosen due to its managed nature, allowing for automatic backups, patching, and scaling. Read replicas will be deployed to distribute the read load across multiple instances, ensuring that the primary database remains responsive. The Application Load Balancer (ALB) will intelligently route read requests to the appropriate read replicas, optimizing performance and reducing latency. Additionally, using Amazon CloudFront as a CDN can help cache static content and reduce the load on the database for frequently accessed data, further improving performance.

The architecture is designed to be scalable; as read demand increases, more read replicas can be added without significant changes to the application. This approach also provides reliability, as the managed nature of RDS means that AWS handles failover and recovery. Cost considerations are also taken into account, as RDS allows for a pay-as-you-go model, enabling the client to scale resources according to actual usage rather than over-provisioning upfront."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
