    "reasoning": "To handle the high transaction volume of 10,000 TPS, using Amazon Kinesis for real-time data streaming allows for efficient ingestion and processing of transaction data. AWS Lambda serves as the compute layer to process each transaction in a serverless manner, providing automatic scaling to meet demand. Additionally, Amazon ECS can be employed for containerized microservices that may require more consistent performance for complex fraud detection algorithms.\n\nFor data storage, Amazon DynamoDB is chosen due to its low-latency performance and ability to scale seamlessly with high request rates. Using Amazon ElastiCache can enhance read performance for frequently accessed data. The combination of these services ensures a reliable architecture that can quickly adapt to varying loads while minimizing costs by avoiding over-provisioning resources.\n\nTo monitor the system, Amazon CloudWatch and AWS X-Ray will provide insights into the application\u2019s performance and help troubleshoot any issues that arise in real-time. This will ensure that the fraud detection system remains reliable and efficient, even under heavy load.",
    "trade_offs": [
      {
        "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS for compute",
        "chose": "AWS Lambda",
        "reason": "AWS Lambda is well-suited for handling variable workloads and provides automatic scaling without the need for server management.",
        "when_to_switch": "If consistent high CPU usage is observed that exceeds Lambda's limits, moving to Amazon ECS for more control over resource allocation would be beneficial."
