      ],
      "monitoring": [
        "Amazon CloudWatch"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "Amazon CloudFront is chosen to distribute content to users with low latency while ensuring a global reach. By integrating Amazon Route 53 for DNS management and AWS Global Accelerator, we can direct traffic to the nearest region, enhancing performance and failover capabilities. AWS Lambda is employed for serverless transcoding tasks, allowing for automatic scaling based on the volume of incoming video uploads, ensuring the system can handle spikes in traffic without incurring unnecessary costs for idle resources.\n\nTo ensure data is stored and managed efficiently, Amazon DynamoDB is utilized as a highly available NoSQL database to keep track of transcoding jobs and user metadata. This choice provides seamless scalability and low-latency performance. Amazon SQS is used for message queuing between the video upload process and the transcoding service, ensuring that jobs are processed reliably and in order. Finally, Amazon CloudWatch is implemented for monitoring the entire pipeline, allowing for proactive management and alerting on performance metrics and error rates."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS for transcoding",
      "chose": "AWS Lambda",
      "reason": "AWS Lambda allows for automatic scaling and is cost-effective as you only pay for the compute time used during the transcoding process. This fits well for sporadic transcoding tasks that can be triggered by user uploads.",
      "when_to_switch": "If transcoding tasks become consistently high in volume and require persistent processing power, switching to Amazon ECS would provide more control over compute resources."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon DynamoDB vs Amazon RDS for metadata storage",
      "chose": "Amazon DynamoDB",
      "reason": "Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database that offers automatic scaling and low latency, suitable for variable workloads and rapidly changing data structures in video transcoding pipelines.",
      "when_to_switch": "If complex transactions and relationships between data entities become necessary, Amazon RDS would be more suitable due to its relational database capabilities."
    },
    {
      "decision": "Amazon Route 53 vs AWS Global Accelerator for traffic management",
      "chose": "AWS Global Accelerator",
      "reason": "AWS Global Accelerator provides improved availability and performance by routing user traffic to optimal AWS endpoints based on health, geography, and routing policies.",
      "when_to_switch": "If the architecture becomes more dependent on static IP addresses for certain services, Amazon Route 53 could be integrated for DNS management alongside Global Accelerator."
