{
  "scenario_summary": "The client needs a highly available AWS infrastructure for processing 1 million PDFs daily in a document processing pipeline.",
  "architecture": {
    "layers": {
      "edge": [
        "Amazon CloudFront"
      ],
      "networking": [
        "Elastic Load Balancer",
        "Amazon API Gateway"
      ],
      "compute": [
        "AWS Lambda",
        "Amazon ECS"
      ],
      "database": [
        "Amazon DynamoDB"
      ],
      "messaging": [
        "Amazon SQS"
      ],
      "monitoring": [
        "Amazon CloudWatch"
      ]
    },
    "reasoning": "Amazon CloudFront is used for distributing the document uploads to users globally, providing low latency. The architecture leverages an Elastic Load Balancer to manage incoming requests and distribute them across AWS Lambda and Amazon ECS, ensuring high availability and scalability. AWS Lambda is ideal for processing events triggered by document uploads, while Amazon ECS can handle more complex processing tasks in containers. Using Amazon DynamoDB allows for a highly available NoSQL database to store processed document metadata, ensuring rapid access and scalability as data grows. Amazon SQS provides a reliable messaging service that decouples the components of the pipeline, improving fault tolerance and allowing for asynchronous processing of documents.\n\nThis architecture is designed to be cost-efficient, as AWS Lambda operates on a pay-as-you-go model, charging only for the compute time consumed. By selecting DynamoDB, the client benefits from its on-demand capacity mode, which can automatically scale to handle varying workloads without upfront costs. The entire system can be monitored using Amazon CloudWatch to ensure that performance metrics and logs are available for troubleshooting and optimization."
  },
  "trade_offs": [
    {
      "decision": "AWS Lambda vs Amazon ECS for processing",
